{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook and the complex, often toxic relationships that fueled its rise. directed by david fincher with razor-sharp precision, the movie transforms what could have been a dry tech origin story into a gripping, psychologically rich drama. the screenplay by aaron sorkin is brilliant—fast-paced, witty, and layered with subtext, every line feels like a calculated strike, echoing the cutthroat nature of the digital age.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as a brilliant but emotionally stunted genius, driven by insecurity and a desperate need for validation. his portrayal is chillingly accurate, capturing the quiet intensity and social awkwardness that defined zuckerberg’s early years. justin timberlake is equally compelling as sean parker, the charismatic, reckless co-founder of napster who brings both inspiration and chaos into zuckerberg’s world. timberlake’s performance is electric, embodying the seductive danger of unchecked ambition.\n\nthe supporting cast, including armie hammer and jesse plemons as the winklevoss twins, is outstanding. the twins are not mere villains but tragic figures—privileged, entitled, yet deeply wronged. the film’s use of digital effects to portray the twins as identical brothers is seamless and adds to the eerie symmetry of their role in the narrative.\n\ndanny elfman’s score is haunting and minimalist, perfectly complementing the film’s cold, modern aesthetic. the editing is brisk and rhythmic, mirroring the pace of code and courtroom proceedings alike. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth is stark and clinical, reinforcing the emotional distance between characters and the impersonal nature of technology.\n\nwhat elevates the film beyond a simple biopic is its thematic depth. it’s not just about facebook—it’s about friendship, betrayal, intellectual theft, and the cost of innovation. the movie questions whether success justifies moral compromise, and whether connection in the digital age is ever truly real. the final scene, with zuckerberg refreshing his ex-girlfriend’s facebook page, is one of the most haunting moments in modern cinema—silent, lonely, and devastating.\n\nthe social network is more than a movie about a website; it’s a portrait of a generation, a warning about the human cost of ambition, and a chillingly accurate depiction of how power is built, lost, and redefined in the 21st century. it’s intelligent, provocative, and unforgettable.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0004889961564913392}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.31129345297813416}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.47485053539276123}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.001073380233719945}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0032333259005099535}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00011812942830147222}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.1336939334869385}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -2.3841830625315197e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.005247865337878466}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.6555284261703491}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.756337511935271e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0002980979916173965}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.8322005271911621}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -9.298280929215252e-06}, {"token": " complex", "logprob": -0.6189194917678833}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.6082777976989746}, {"token": " often", "logprob": -0.010495230555534363}, {"token": " toxic", "logprob": -1.2809041738510132}, {"token": " relationships", "logprob": -0.1013792231